# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYTE-PIN SUBJECTS -- owner decision D7 (giga plan 2026-08-22, line 212)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT THE "CRLF DAEMON" ACTUALLY IS. Not a process: `core.autocrlf` is true on
# this host, so every fresh checkout translates LF to CRLF, while a file whose
# OWN BYTES are hashed into an identity was pinned on the LF form. The pin then
# fails on a Windows worktree and passes on Linux from the same commit. Four
# recorded appearances (docs/GATE0_OWNER_DECISIONS_20260817.md sections 5 and 6,
# and the Wave-1 "fixtures stop writing CRLF into byte-exact files" family). It
# recurs because the protection was a hand-maintained per-file list and every
# new byte-pin subject arrives unlisted.
#
# The list below is therefore paired with tests/test_byte_pin_eol_durability.py,
# which re-derives the census from the source and fails when a subject is not
# covered here. Adding a file that hashes its own bytes without adding a line
# here is a test failure, not a Windows-only surprise three sessions later.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-22 on this worktree, `git ls-files --eol`: of the 12 byte-pin
# subjects, 2 carried an attribute and 4 were checked out CRLF.
#
# Setting `-text` changes no committed byte -- every subject is already `i/lf`.
# It only stops the checkout translation. Files already CRLF in an existing
# worktree stay CRLF until they are re-checked-out or renormalised; that is a
# working-tree operation, deliberately not performed here.
#
# NARROWER THAN THE ALTERNATIVE, ON PURPOSE. `*.py -text` would end the family
# for every Python file at once (measured blast radius: 947 tracked files are
# CRLF in this worktree, all `i/lf`, so the index would not change at all). That
# is a whole-repository EOL policy and belongs to the owner, not to this list.

# Retained, non-importable package resource verified against its exact Git blob
# SHA-1 in daedalus/kairos/gated_writes.py. A CRLF checkout makes the module
# unimportable, which is fail-closed but is an availability break, not a finding.
*.py.src -text

